WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.206
Python applications may receive file-related values from many places:
user-supplied filenames, uploaded files, command-line arguments,
configuration, API requests, archive member names, generated filenames,
and database records.

2
00:00:13.206 --> 00:00:15.418
Each source has a different trust level.

3
00:00:15.418 --> 00:00:24.656
A value that looks like a normal filename may still contain unexpected
separators, encodings, reserved names, confusing characters, or context-
dependent behavior.

4
00:00:24.656 --> 00:00:28.039
Absolute and relative paths should be handled deliberately.

5
00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:34.740
A safe design usually defines a base directory or allowlisted storage
location, then constrains operations to that area.

6
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:42.741
Canonicalization concepts help the application reason about the final
resolved location before opening, writing, serving, or deleting a file.

7
00:00:42.741 --> 00:00:50.288
The stable principle is not to trust a path string until the application
has confirmed where it leads and whether that location is allowed.

8
00:00:50.288 --> 00:00:56.598
Generated server-side filenames are often safer than storing user-provided
names directly as filesystem names.

9
00:00:56.598 --> 00:01:03.819
The original filename can be preserved as metadata for display when
needed, but storage names should avoid relying on user control.

10
00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:12.537
Extensions may be useful for user experience or routing, but they should
not be the only control for content trust, parser choice, or authorization
decisions.

11
00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:20.083
Symbolic links and mounted directories deserve attention at a high level
because they can make a path behave differently than it appears.

12
00:01:20.083 --> 00:01:28.735
The application should constrain file access to intended directories and
intended operations, and it should avoid letting callers choose arbitrary
paths.

13
00:01:28.735 --> 00:01:34.200
Safe path design is about reducing ambiguity before a file operation
reaches the operating system.
